\subsection{ASV Diagnostic Results}
\label{sec:asv_diagnostics}

For HumAID ($K{=}10$, 56 scenarios), 55.4\% are flagged as magnitude-ASV-unsafe: IV, mediation, front-door, and exposure all trigger, while confounding, collider, and M-bias remain below 10\% bias (Figure~\ref{fig:asv_heatmap}). For CivilComments ($K{=}2$), 100\% of scenarios are ASV-unsafe despite $F_1$ ranging from $0.69$ (7B) to $0.82$ (frontier).

Overlaying all 23 empirical matrices onto the theoretical Dirichlet violation-rate curves (Figure~\ref{fig:dirichlet_overlay}) confirms placement: VAST in the danger zone, HumAID mid-range, CivilComments approaching safe for exposure but still in IV amplification.

\subsection{ASV Stability Across DGP Parameters}
\label{sec:asv_stability}

We test whether ASV rankings are stable across different data-generating process (DGP) configurations by varying the treatment-effect vector $\boldsymbol{\beta}$ across three settings: alternating-large ($\boldsymbol{\beta}=[0,\,1.0,\,-0.5]$), same-sign ($\boldsymbol{\beta}=[0,\,0.5,\,0.3]$), and alternating-small ($\boldsymbol{\beta}=[0,\,0.2,\,-0.1]$), with $K{=}3$, $\tau{=}0.5$, and five $C_0$ diagonal values $\in\{0.5,0.6,0.7,0.8,0.9\}$.

\input{exp_e_asv_stability_table.tex}

Three patterns emerge (Table~\ref{tab:asv_stability}). First, IV magnitude ASV is identical across all three DGPs ($\delta^*{=}0.203$). This is a mathematical identity, not an empirical finding: the Wald estimator $\hat{\tau}_{\text{IV}} = \text{RF}/\text{FS}$ has $\boldsymbol{\beta}$ in both numerator and denominator, and the ratio depends only on $C$ and the first-stage structure, making ASV invariant to $\boldsymbol{\beta}$ by construction. Second, exposure ASV depends on the coefficient ratio structure: alternating-large ($\beta_2/\beta_1 = -0.5$) and alternating-small ($\beta_2/\beta_1 = -0.5$) share the same ratio and yield identical $\delta^*{=}0.090$, while same-sign ($\beta_2/\beta_1 = 0.6$) gives $\delta^*{=}0.223$; thus the experiment provides two distinct sign-structure conditions, not three. Third, confounding ASV depends on effect magnitudes: only alternating-large triggers the 10\% threshold ($\delta^*{=}0.348$); smaller effects never reach 10\% bias.

\subsection{PPI++ Gold Label Ratio Sensitivity}
\label{sec:ppi_gold_ratio}

We test PPI++ correction performance as a function of gold label fraction ($K{=}3$, $N{=}10{,}000$, 100 MC replications, 5 Dirichlet confusion matrices with $\min_k C_{kk}{\geq}0.6$).

Negative values indicate PPI++ increases RMSE relative to the naive estimator.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{PPI++ RMSE reduction (\%) vs.\ gold label fraction.}
\label{tab:ppi_gold_ratio}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Topology & 5\% & 10\% & 20\% & 50\% & 100\% \\
\midrule
Confounding & $+$84.8 & $+$88.6 & $+$91.8 & $+$94.2 & $+$95.2 \\
Exposure & $+$75.2 & $+$83.1 & $+$87.1 & $+$91.2 & $+$93.8 \\
Mediation & $+$56.7 & $+$70.4 & $+$77.2 & $+$85.3 & $+$88.7 \\
IV & $+$1.4 & $+$22.1 & $+$50.1 & $+$66.7 & $+$76.4 \\
M-bias & $-$125.3 & $-$43.6 & $-$19.6 & $+$6.0 & $+$18.5 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

For confounding and exposure, PPI++ achieves $>$75\% RMSE reduction even at 5\% gold labels; IV requires ${\geq}20\%$ gold to reach 50\% reduction, reflecting the Wald estimator's higher variance. M-bias exhibits harmful correction at ${\leq}20\%$ gold ($-125\%$ at 5\%), because PPI++ re-exposes the M-bias pathway that annotation error had attenuated; only at ${\geq}50\%$ gold does the estimator become weakly beneficial ($+6\%$).

\subsection{Realistic Differential Misclassification Thresholds}
\label{sec:realistic_epsilon}

Our non-differential assumption (Definition~\ref{def:nondiff}) requires $P(A^*{\mid}A,Y) = P(A^*{\mid}A)$. We estimate how much $Y$-stratified differential error each of our 23 empirical LLM confusion matrices can tolerate before exposure sign reversal exceeds 5\%. For each matrix $C$, we construct $C_{\text{high}} = \text{renorm}(C + \varepsilon\Delta)$ and $C_{\text{low}} = \text{renorm}(C - \varepsilon\Delta)$ with random off-diagonal perturbation $\Delta$ (500 MC trials per $\varepsilon$), sweep $\varepsilon \in [0, 0.50]$, and report the safe threshold $\varepsilon^*_{\text{safe}} = \max\{\varepsilon : \text{reversal rate} < 5\%\}$ (Table~\ref{tab:realistic_epsilon}).

For $K{=}2$ (binary), no configuration produces sign reversal up to $\varepsilon{=}0.50$. For $K{\geq}3$, the majority of configurations are fragile ($\varepsilon^*_{\text{safe}}{=}0$), confirming that the non-differential assumption is load-bearing for multi-class exposure.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Safe differential misclassification thresholds $\varepsilon^*_{\text{safe}}$ by dataset/$K$.}
\label{tab:realistic_epsilon}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Dataset} & $K$ & $n$ & \textbf{Mean} $\varepsilon^*$ & \textbf{Median} $\varepsilon^*$ & $\varepsilon^*{=}0$ \\
\midrule
Civil Comments & 2 & 7 & 0.50 & 0.50 & 0/7 \\
VAST & 3 & 8 & 0.003 & 0.00 & 7/8 \\
HuMAID & 10 & 8 & 0.045 & 0.00 & 7/8 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

The distribution is sharply bimodal: all $K{=}2$ configurations are immune to differential misclassification within the tested range ($\varepsilon^*_{\text{safe}}{=}0.50$), consistent with the binary sign-preservation guarantee (Proposition~\ref{prop:signflip}(c)). For $K{\geq}3$, 14 of 16 configurations have $\varepsilon^*_{\text{safe}}{=}0$: sign reversal occurs at the smallest tested $\varepsilon{=}0.01$. Two outliers (VAST: $\varepsilon^*{=}0.02$; HuMAID: $\varepsilon^*{=}0.36$) have atypically structured confusion matrices. This confirms that even small violations of non-differential misclassification are consequential for multi-class exposure, underscoring the practical importance of validating $P(A^*{\mid}A,Y) \approx P(A^*{\mid}A)$ on gold-labeled subsets when $K \geq 3$.

\subsection{Proportional Effects with Empirical Confusion Matrices}
\label{sec:proportional_real_matrices}

Theorem~\ref{thm:monotone} assumes proportional effects ($f = \lambda g$). We evaluate this assumption using all 23 empirical LLM confusion matrices under four DGP groups in the mediation topology (23 matrices $\times$ 50 seeds $\times$ 100{,}000 precomputed samples per seed).

Evaluation uses the mediation DGP. $\lambda(C) \in [0,1]$: proportion satisfying the Theorem~\ref{thm:monotone} bound. Sign pres.: sign preservation rate of the direct effect estimate.

