\subsection{Case Study: ASV Diagnosis of Political Ad Tone Classification}
\label{sec:asv_case_study}

We illustrate the ASV diagnostic workflow on a concrete CSS application. \citet{egami_hinck_stewart_wei2023} use LLM classifiers to annotate political advertising tone ($K{=}3$: promote/contrast/attack) from the Fowler \& Ridout database, then estimate the causal effect of ad tone on voter outcomes. The annotated variable is the \textbf{exposure} (treatment). Using a representative $K{=}3$ confusion matrix from our empirical set (VAST DeepSeek zero-shot, mean diagonal 0.734) as a proxy:

\begin{center}
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Coefficient & $\delta^*_{\text{mag}}$ (10\%) & $\delta^*_{\text{sf}}$ & Relative bias at $\delta{=}1$ \\
\midrule
$\beta_1$ (primary) & 0.234 & $> 1.5$ & 39.7\% \\
$\beta_2$ (secondary) & 0.154 & $> 1.5$ & 60.4\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{center}

Both coefficients are magnitude-unsafe ($\delta^* < 1$), with the secondary coefficient more fragile ($\delta^*{=}0.154$). If the same annotator were instead classifying a \textbf{confounder} (e.g., media framing as a control variable), magnitude ASV rises to $\delta^*{=}0.690$, a $3{\times}$ increase in error tolerance from the same confusion matrix. This demonstrates the central insight: topology, not annotator quality, determines vulnerability. Algorithm~\ref{alg:correction} routes this exposure topology to PPI++ or DSL correction; matrix-based methods (naive plug-in, MLA) are ineffective due to FWL non-applicability.

\subsection{End-to-End Demonstration}
\label{sec:e2e_demo}

\input{e2e_demo_table.tex}

The demonstration uses a symmetric $C_0$ ($d_{\mathrm{rel}}{=}0$); empirical $K{=}3$ matrices deviate substantially (median $d_{\mathrm{rel}}{=}0.33$, Remark~\ref{rem:symmetry}), but core ASV ranking and correction routing remain valid under Conjecture~\ref{conj:monotone}.

\subsection{Semi-Real Case Study with Production LLM Confusion Matrices}
\label{sec:real_cm_demo}

We repeat the end-to-end pipeline using 10 confusion matrices estimated from production LLM annotation of VAST stance detection ($K{=}3$; DeepSeek, Llama4, Qwen2.5-7B, Qwen3 under zero-shot and few-shot prompting). These matrices exhibit median $d_{\mathrm{rel}}{=}0.33$ (Remark~\ref{rem:symmetry}), far from the symmetric assumption of Theorem~\ref{thm:k3_symmetric}. Results confirm both topology-dependent patterns: PPI++ achieves 93.6\% median exposure bias reduction while plug-in corrections yield 0\% improvement for confounding (FWL invariance, machine precision; Table~\ref{tab:e2e-real-cm}).

\input{e2e_real_cm_table.tex}

\subsection{Correction Method Sensitivity to $K$}
\label{sec:correction_k_sensitivity}

We test whether the correction method ranking from Table~\ref{tab:correction} ($K{=}3$) is stable at $K{=}5$ and $K{=}10$. For each $K$, we sample 5 random Dirichlet confusion matrices (diagonal $\geq 0.6$), run 100 MC replications per topology--matrix combination ($N{=}5{,}000$, 10\% gold fraction), and compute RMSE reduction for all five methods across all seven topologies.

\paragraph{Grand ranking stability.}

